CORE
main.cpp
--cpp98
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ cross-cast: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ cross-cast, deeper: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ downcast to an intermediate class: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ downcast failing: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ private base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ no such base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.7\] line \d+ null: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.8\] line \d+ from the second base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.9\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.dynamic.cast]/9-10: dynamic_cast<C *>(v) with C not a base
of v's class yields a pointer to the C subobject of the most derived
object, if unique and public (a down- or cross-cast), else null; a null v
yields null (/4).  The front end returned the operand statically adjusted
(downcasts) or a nondeterministic choice between the unadjusted pointer
and null (cross-casts), so failing casts succeeded and cross-casts pointed
to the wrong subobject (bin53).  The check is now resolved once every
class of the translation unit is known: for each class D with a unique A
subobject, the most derived object is a D iff the A subobject's vtable
pointer holds the value D installs, and the result is then the C
subobject or null.  A pointer matching no class of the translation unit
(an object of a class defined elsewhere) and layouts with virtual bases or
repeated subobjects keep the nondeterministic model.  g++ 13 and clang++
18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
